  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The extreme value theorem states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nSo, the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe smallest possible integer $h$ such that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[h,3]$ is $h=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14211274/assets/ab92ed0c59facac4fccb050734aba133474c8a0585c6bd981abe7ea05ed0e6be.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
